  "family": "Fabaceae",
  "memo": "Western Redbud is a commonly used native shrub, commonly found in riparian canyons. It is usually sold as a multi-trunk shrub, rather than a single trunk tree standard, but can be trained into either a single or multi-trunk tree, where it is effective as a flowering accent. It tolerates dry conditions, but exhibits more vigorous, lush growth with regular deep watering. It blooms best in full sun. Otherwise, it is quite reliable once established, except for occasional tent caterpillar attacks, which can be controlled with spraying. It is resistant to oak root fungus.",
